## Proof that the total variation of $f(t)=\frac{\sin(\pi t)}{\pi t}$ is infinite

**Setup.** Let $f(t)=\frac{\sin(\pi t)}{\pi t}$ (with $f(0)=1$ by continuity). Then
$$f'(t)=\frac{\pi t\cos(\pi t)-\sin(\pi t)}{\pi t^{2}},$$
so the total variation on $\mathbb{R}$ is $V(f)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|f'(t)|\,dt$ (since $f$ is absolutely continuous on every finite interval, and $V=\lim_{R\to\infty}\int_{-R}^{R}|f'|$ by monotone convergence). We show $V(f)=\infty$.

**Strategy.** For any partition $t_1<t_2<\cdots<t_N$, the variation satisfies $V(f)\ge\sum_{n=1}^{N-1}|f(t_{n+1})-f(t_n)|$. We produce points $t_n\to\infty$ at which $f$ takes values of alternating sign and magnitude $\asymp 1/n$; the resulting sum is a divergent harmonic series.

**Locating the critical points.** The critical points of $f$ satisfy $f'(t)=0$, i.e.
$$\pi t\cos(\pi t)=\sin(\pi t)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \tan(\pi t)=\pi t \quad(\cos(\pi t)\neq 0).$$
For each integer $n\ge 1$, consider the interval $(n,\,n+\tfrac12)$. There $\tan(\pi t)$ increases continuously from $0$ to $+\infty$ while $\pi t$ increases from $\pi n$ to $\pi(n+\tfrac12)$, so by the intermediate value theorem there is a unique $t_n\in(n,n+\tfrac12)$ with $\tan(\pi t_n)=\pi t_n$. Writing $u_n:=t_n-n\in(0,\tfrac12)$, the equation becomes
$$\tan(\pi u_n)=\pi(n+u_n). \tag{1}$$

**Value of $f$ at $t_n$.** From $\sin(\pi t_n)=\pi t_n\cos(\pi t_n)$ we get
$$f(t_n)=\frac{\sin(\pi t_n)}{\pi t_n}=\cos(\pi t_n)=\cos(\pi n+\pi u_n)=(-1)^n\cos(\pi u_n).$$
Since $u_n\in(0,\tfrac12)$, we have $\cos(\pi u_n)>0$, so $\operatorname{sgn} f(t_n)=(-1)^n$: **the signs alternate**.

**Lower bound on $|f(t_n)|$.** Set $\varepsilon_n:=\tfrac12-u_n\in(0,\tfrac12)$. Then $\cos(\pi u_n)=\sin(\pi\varepsilon_n)$, and (1) becomes $\cot(\pi\varepsilon_n)=\pi(n+\tfrac12-\varepsilon_n)$, i.e.
$$\tan(\pi\varepsilon_n)=\frac{1}{\pi\bigl(n+\tfrac12-\varepsilon_n\bigr)}. \tag{2}$$
For $n\ge 1$, the right-hand side is $\le 1/\pi<1=\tan(\pi/4)$, hence $\pi\varepsilon_n<\pi/4$, so $\varepsilon_n<1/4$. Using $\tan x\le 2x$ for $x\in(0,\pi/4)$ (since $x\mapsto\tan x/x$ is increasing and $\tan(\pi/4)/(\pi/4)=4/\pi<2$) together with $\tan x\ge x$:
$$\pi\varepsilon_n\;\le\;\tan(\pi\varepsilon_n)=\frac{1}{\pi(n+\tfrac12-\varepsilon_n)}\;\le\;\frac{1}{\pi n}, \qquad
\tan(\pi\varepsilon_n)\;\le\;2\pi\varepsilon_n \;\Longrightarrow\; \varepsilon_n\ge\frac{1}{2\pi^{2}(n+\tfrac12)}.$$
(For the upper bound we used $\tan x\ge x$; for the lower, $\tan x\le 2x$.) Now use $\sin x\ge \tfrac{2}{\pi}x$ for $x\in[0,\pi/2]$:
$$|f(t_n)|=\sin(\pi\varepsilon_n)\ge \frac{2}{\pi}\cdot\pi\varepsilon_n=2\varepsilon_n\ge\frac{1}{\pi^{2}(n+\tfrac12)}\ge\frac{1}{2\pi^{2}\,n}, \qquad n\ge 1. \tag{3}$$

**Divergence of the variation.** Since $t_1<t_2<\cdots$ and the signs $(-1)^n$ alternate, consecutive values have opposite sign, so
$$|f(t_{n+1})-f(t_n)|=|f(t_{n+1})|+|f(t_n)|\ge |f(t_n)|\ge\frac{1}{2\pi^{2}n}.$$
Therefore, for every $N\ge 2$,
$$V(f)\;\ge\;\sum_{n=1}^{N-1}|f(t_{n+1})-f(t_n)|\;\ge\;\frac{1}{2\pi^{2}}\sum_{n=1}^{N-1}\frac{1}{n}\;\xrightarrow[N\to\infty]{}\;\infty.$$

**Conclusion.** The total variation is infinite, and consequently
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\left|\frac{\pi t\cos(\pi t)-\sin(\pi t)}{\pi t^{2}}\right|dt \;=\; V(f) \;=\; \infty.$$

